HTML
Q1..What is HTML and why is it used?
Answer: HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the standard language to create web pages. It defines the structure of a webpage using elements like headings, paragraphs, links, and forms.

Q2..What is the difference between HTML and XHTML?
Answer: XHTML is stricter than HTML. It must be well-formed, all tags closed, lowercase, and nested properly. HTML is more lenient.
“HTML is flexible and forgiving with syntax, while XHTML is strict, follows XML rules, and requires proper closing of all tags.”

Q3.What are semantic tags in HTML?
Answer: Semantic tags provide meaning to content, helping browsers and search engines understand it. Examples: <header>, <footer>, <article>, <section>.

Q4..Difference between block-level and inline elements
Block-level → take full width, start on a new line (e.g., <div>, <p>)
Inline → take only the space they need, stay in line (e.g., <span>, <a>)

Q5.What is the difference between <div> and <span>?
Answer: <div> is a block-level container, <span> is inline. Both are used to group content but behave differently in layout.

Q6..What is the purpose of the <meta> tag?
Answer: <meta> provides metadata about the page, such as charset, description, keywords, viewport, and SEO info.

Q7..What are forms in HTML?
Answer: Forms collect user input through fields like <input>, <textarea>, <select> and send data to a server using GET or POST methods.

Q8..Difference between GET and POST in forms
GET → sends data in URL, visible, limited length, not secure
POST → sends data in request body, more secure, unlimited length

Q9..What is the difference between HTML5 and HTML4?
Answer: HTML5 introduced semantic tags, audio/video support, canvas, offline storage, and better forms. HTML4 is older and less feature-rich.

Q10..What are the new input types in HTML5?
Answer: Examples: email, number, url, date, range, color, tel, search. 

Q11.What is the difference between <iframe> and <embed>?
Answer: <iframe> embeds another HTML page inside the current page. <embed> embeds external content like PDFs, videos, or plugins.

Q12..What is the purpose of the <canvas> tag?
Answer: <canvas> is used to draw graphics dynamically using JavaScript, like charts, animations, or games.

Q13..Difference between <section>, <article>, and <div>
<section> → groups related content
<article> → independent, self-contained content
<div> → generic container with no meaning

Q14..What are data- attributes?*
Answer: Custom attributes starting with data- allow storing extra information on HTML elements. Example: <div data-id="123"></div>

Q15..What is the difference between <ul>, <ol>, and <dl>?
<ul> → unordered list

<ol> → ordered list

<dl> → definition list

Q16..What is the difference between <b> and <strong>?
Answer: <b> is for visual bold text. <strong> indicates importance and is semantic.

Q17..Explain the difference between <i> and <em>
Answer: <i> is for visual italics. <em> indicates emphasis (semantic).

Q18..What is the difference between <link> and <a>?
Answer: <link> is used to link external resources like CSS. <a> creates clickable hyperlinks.

Q19..What is the difference between inline, internal, and external CSS?
Inline → style attribute in HTML tag

Internal → <style> in <head>

External → separate CSS file linked via <link>

Q20..What is the purpose of the viewport meta tag?
It controls the page’s layout on mobile devices. Example:

<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">














